\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Report on 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Returns to Public vs.
 Private College: RD Evidence from Centralized Admissions
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Using national IDs, the authors combine large adminstrative datasets from
 Taiwan, including birth outcomes, college applications, admissions outcomes,
 and eventual earnings, in order to examine the returns from attending a
 public college versus a private college.
 The Taiwanese government maintains a centrally administrated college admissions
 system, so the authors exploit the discontinuities generated by this procedure.
 They take advantage of the exogenous variation by utilizing a stacked regressio
n discontinuity design.
 The paper estimates the monetary returns for students who are on the margin
 for admissions into either a national or private college within each college
 major.
 In the paper, the authors explicitly state the following identifying assumption
 of the RD design: the observable characteristics of those just above or
 just below a particular score cutoff are smooth across the threshold.
 Table 2 provides the results from the first-stage and reduced-form regressions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The authors admit that a significant limitation of their study is that the
 Labor data can be matched to only about 33 percent of those in the MOE
 records.
 Even though the authors provide explanation for this occurence, I still
 believe that the data limitation signals some element of selection.
 The most concerning aspect is the mandatory 18-month national military
 service that is mandatory for males.
 Panel A of Figure 4 does not provide compelling evidence that there is
 balance in probability of showing up in the Labor data.
 It seems as though there is an evident discontinuity at the cutoff caused
 by a sudden kink in the slope.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In addition, Panels B-E of Figure 4 do not provide enough sufficeint evidence
 that other observable characteristics are continuous.
 The most damaging figure is Panel C, which seems to show a clear discontinuity
 of males at the cutoff.
 It would have been benefical for the authors to provide corresponding regressio
ns discontinuity estimates of the predetermined characteristics along with
 the figures.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Next, another test of the identifying assumption the authors could have
 performed would be to show the RD estimates with and without controls.
 If the test shows that estimates change in a significant way, then there
 may be manipulation around the cutoff.
 Furthermore the authors could look for discontinuities in the outcome at
 fake cutoffs.
 This is most impertative when considering the fact that different schools
 employ different cutoffs, and the authors employ a stacked RD design.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Moving on to the robustness tests, I have a concern with the way the authors
 manipulate the bandwidth and functional form.
 To elaborate, as the authors increase the bandwidths, they also alter the
 functional form that determines the flexible fit.
 This could affect the estimates of the indicator function.
 Instead, I would suggest altering the bandwidth independently of the functional
 form.
 A better robustness test would be to show the results from multiple functional
 forms for each of the three bandwidths.
\end_layout

